course: dictionary
lesson: 9279
title: Spaniard
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A Spaniard is a person from Spain. It is a noun, so it can change to show more than one: one Spaniard, many Spaniards. Read the word with that meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «In 1532 Francisco Pizarro captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa, who filled a room with gold to buy his freedom, but the Spaniards killed him anyway.» The clues are that the Spaniards came with Pizarro, captured Atahualpa and then killed him. Swap in "people from Spain" and the sentence still works, so that is the meaning.
explain: Be careful not to mix up the words. Spaniard is a person, and Spanish is the language, as in "Spanish food". In history lessons, Christopher Columbus was Italian, but his crews were Spaniards. Today, Spaniards speak Spanish, Catalan, Basque and Galician.
fact: A Spaniard is a person from Spain.
fact: The plural of Spaniard is Spaniards.
fact: In «the Spaniards killed him anyway», the Spaniards are the men from Spain who captured and killed Atahualpa.
fact: Spaniards speak Spanish, Catalan, Basque and Galician.
fact: Spanish is the language, and Spaniard is the person.
exercise: In «the Spaniards killed him anyway», who are the Spaniards? | Emperors who filled rooms with gold | Horses that carried Pizarro | Gold makers from the Inca city | *People from Spain
exercise: Which word means a person from Spain? | Spanner | Spain | *Spaniard | Spanish
exercise: What is the plural of Spaniard? | Spaniardes | *Spaniards | Spanishes | Spaniard's
test: Which word names the language, not the person? | Spaniard | *Spanish | Spain | Spaniards
test: Christopher Columbus was Italian, but some of his crews were what? | Explorers from the Americas | *Spaniards | Emperors | Italians
exam: Which of these is a person, not a language? | *Spaniard | Basque | Catalan | Galician
